# Introduction

Low concentrations can increase a measured biological response before higher concentrations reduce it, a pattern termed hormesis [@calabrese2008]. For a threshold hormesis equation, the `nec` parameter identifies the peak of that rise, where the fitted response differs from the control. Its interpretation differs from the breakpoint of a monotone threshold equation. `bayesnec` calculates a no-significant-effect concentration (NSEC) on the declining limb, using a lower quantile of the posterior control response as the reference [@fisherfox2023].

Australian and New Zealand guideline methods prefer defensible NECs and NSECs as chronic inputs, although their NSEC definition describes a monotonically decreasing model [@warne2025]. Guidance for stimulated responses from Environment Canada and OECD TG 201 supports retaining the observations, fitting an appropriate curve and assessing inhibition relative to the control [@canada2005stats; @oecd2026tg201]. The post-peak NSEC extends that control-based comparison to hormetic curves. It is an interpolated statistical no-effect estimate and can have a nonzero associated effect, as a NOEC can [@fisherfox2023; @fisher2023ieam]. This example shows how to obtain and assess it with `bayesnec`, using marine growth rates and lettuce biomass.

# Methods

## Control-based locations

For an endpoint where a decrease represents poorer performance, let $m(c)$ be the fitted mean at concentration $c$, with $m(0)$ the fitted unexposed control. The peak is the maximum fitted response, and the control return is the first crossing of $m(0)$ after that peak. A control-based ECx is a specified percentage decline from $m(0)$; the post-peak NSEC is the first descending crossing of a lower posterior quantile of the control response. These locations differ even when estimated from one equation (Figure 1).

```{r estimand-plot, fig.width=6, fig.height=4, fig.cap="Figure 1. A theoretical hormetic curve, $m(c)=1+0.25c\\exp(1-c)-0.15\\max(c-1,0)$, showing the four candidate locations, with each concentration given in the legend. The grey dashed lines mark the control response $m(0)=1$, a lower reference of 0.95 standing in for a lower posterior quantile of the control, and the EC10 target of 0.9."}
m <- function(z) 1 + 0.25 * z * exp(1 - z) - 0.15 * pmax(z - 1, 0)
x <- seq(0, 4, length.out = 400)
crossing <- function(target) uniroot(function(z) m(z) - target, c(1.01, 4))$root
locations <- c(Peak = 1, `Control return` = crossing(1),
               `Post-peak NSEC` = crossing(0.95), EC10 = crossing(0.9))
op <- par(mar = c(4.3, 4.4, 1, 0.5))
plot(x, m(x), type = "l", lwd = 2, xlab = "Relative concentration",
     ylab = "Mean response", ylim = c(0.55, 1.3))
abline(h = c(1, 0.95, 0.9), lty = 2, col = "grey60")
text(4, c(1, 0.95, 0.9), c("Control", "Lower reference", "EC10 target"),
     adj = c(1, -0.3), cex = 0.7, col = "grey40")
segments(locations, 0.55, locations, m(locations), lty = 1:4 + 1)
legend("topright", legend = sprintf("%s (%.2f)", names(locations), locations),
       lty = 1:4 + 1, bty = "n", cex = 0.8)
par(op)
```

In `bayesnec`, `curve_params()` returns the sampled `nec` parameter for a threshold hormesis equation, while `nec()` returns the NSEC as its default no-effect estimate. `nsec()` uses `sig_val` to set the control reference, and `ecnsec()` gives the percentage effect associated with an NSEC. The NSEC uses the fitted curve between tested concentrations, whereas a NOEC must be one of the tested concentrations [@fisherfox2023]. For example, compare a control-based NSEC and its associated ECNSEC across candidate equations. A posterior draw without stimulation can cross the reference without an intervening peak, so the equation and reported location should both be identified.

## Observations

The [public workbook](https://data.aims.gov.au/data-download/615e208d-f6c7-4b2f-b99e-d4fc50614e48/Negri_etal_2021_Experimental-data_Toxicity-thresholds-gas-condensate-oils.xlsx) accompanying @negri2021condensate contains 65 individual growth rates of *Cladocopium goreaui*: ten unexposed controls and five replicates at each of 11 concentrations [@aims2020condensatedata]. The workbook codes controls at 1 $\mu$g/L total aromatic hydrocarbons (TAH). We subtracted this source value and divided by 1000 to express concentrations in mg/L relative to the source-coded control. The workbook is [CC BY-NC 3.0 Australia](https://researchdata.edu.au/condensate-toxicity-testing/1702929), so `data-raw/hormesis_negri.R` imports the individual observations locally without redistributing them.

The `drc::lettuce` data contain 14 shoot-biomass observations at seven isobutyl alcohol concentrations, with two observations per concentration [@vanewijk1993; @Ritz2016]. The observations are included in `inst/extdata/hormesis_lettuce_1993.csv` (Figure 2). This small terrestrial assay provides a fitting example, not an aquatic guideline input.

```{r lettuce-plot, fig.width=6, fig.height=4, fig.cap="Figure 2. The 14 individual lettuce shoot biomass observations. The concentration axis is log10(concentration + 0.1), and the line joins treatment means for visual orientation; it is not a fitted model."}
lettuce <- read.csv(system.file("extdata", "hormesis_lettuce_1993.csv",
                                package = "bayesnec"))
op <- par(mar = c(4.3, 4.4, 1, 0.5))
plot(lettuce$weight ~ log10(lettuce$conc + 0.1), pch = 16,
     col = "#A25D3A88", xaxt = "n", xlab = "Isobutyl alcohol (mg/L)",
     ylab = "Shoot biomass")
axis(1, at = log10(sort(unique(lettuce$conc)) + 0.1),
     labels = sort(unique(lettuce$conc)), cex.axis = 0.75)
with(aggregate(weight ~ conc, lettuce, mean),
     lines(log10(conc + 0.1), weight, lwd = 2))
par(op)
```

## Fitting and checks

We fitted Gaussian responses with an identity link. The marine candidate set included monotone `neclin` and five hormesis equations; the lettuce set included the five hormesis equations. Lettuce concentration was entered as `log(concentration + 0.1)` because its recorded values span more than two orders of magnitude; the offset retains the control. All returned concentrations below are in mg/L. We retained equations with Rhat at most 1.01, minimum effective sample size at least 400 and no divergent transitions. We replaced unreliable observation-wise leave-one-out scores with exact refits for lettuce before calculating pseudo-BMA model weights [@vehtari2017]. The [research compendium](https://github.com/open-AIMS/hormesis-nsec-compendium) contains the fitting calls and the sampler screen.

The following calls read the saved, screened sets produced by the compendium script and show how to obtain weights, no-effect estimates and their associated effect sizes. The chunk is not evaluated during the vignette build because the marine individual observations are not redistributed with the package.

```{r case-fits, eval=FALSE}
library(bayesnec)
# From the compendium root, after analysis/hormesis_case_fits.R
marine_set <- readRDS("ignore/hormesis_data/marine_set_final.rds")
lettuce_set <- readRDS("ignore/hormesis_data/lettuce_set_final.rds")
summary(marine_set, check_fit = FALSE)$mod_weights
nec(marine_set) # N(S)EC from a NEC and hormesis NSECs
summary(lettuce_set, check_fit = FALSE)$mod_weights
nsec(lettuce_set, sig_val = 0.01)
autoplot(marine_set, nec = FALSE)
autoplot(lettuce_set, nec = FALSE)

fit_marine <- readRDS("ignore/hormesis_data/fit_final_marine_neclinhorme.rds")
curve_params(fit_marine) # sampled peak parameter
nsec_marine <- nsec(fit_marine, sig_val = 0.01)
attr(nsec_marine, "ecnsec_relativeP") # paired ECNSEC
fit_lettuce <- readRDS("ignore/hormesis_data/fit_final_lettuce_nechorme.rds")
nsec_lettuce <- nsec(fit_lettuce, sig_val = 0.01)
attr(nsec_lettuce, "ecnsec_relativeP")
```

# Fitted examples

## *Cladocopium goreaui* growth rates

The ten marine controls had a mean growth rate of 0.108 d^-1^. Treatment means at 0.58 and 1.17 mg/L total aromatic hydrocarbons (TAH) were 22% and 18% above that mean, followed by a decline at higher concentrations. For the retained `neclinhorme` equation, the sampled peak was 0.92 mg/L TAH (95% credible interval 0.56–1.46), while its NSEC was 2.00 mg/L (1.80–2.18) and its paired ECNSEC was 10.6% (2.2–17.8%; Table 2). The NSEC is on the declining limb, between the 1.75 and 2.33 mg/L treatments; the peak parameter describes stimulation instead.

| Equation | Pseudo-BMA weight | Fitted control (d^-1^) | NSEC, mg/L TAH (95% CrI) | ECNSEC, % (95% CrI) | EC10, mg/L TAH (95% CrI) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `neclin` | 0.002 | 0.119 | 1.71 (1.42–2.04) | 7.9 (1.4–13.2) | 1.77 (1.49–2.23) |
| `neclinhorme` | 0.191 | 0.111 | 2.00 (1.80–2.18) | 10.6 (2.2–17.8) | 1.98 (1.65–2.28) |
| `nechorme` | <0.001 | 0.109 | 2.90 (2.73–3.15) | 7.9 (1.2–13.3) | 2.91 (2.75–3.17) |
| `ecxhormebc4` | 0.031 | 0.112 | 2.33 (1.82–2.66) | 6.9 (0.9–12.4) | 2.50 (2.08–2.85) |
| `ecxhormebc5` | 0.776 | 0.114 | 2.09 (1.73–2.41) | 8.4 (1.6–13.7) | 2.20 (1.74–2.57) |

Table 2. Estimates from the five equations retained for the marine growth rates. ECNSEC is paired with each posterior draw of the NSEC. The monotone `neclin` equation usually returns its sampled NEC through `nec()`; its NSEC is shown here for a common control-based comparison. The fitted control is the posterior median at zero concentration, against an observed control mean of 0.108 d^-1^.

```{r marine-fit-plot, echo=FALSE, out.width="100%", fig.cap="Figure 3. Model-averaged fitted mean and five retained candidate equations (blue), with 95% credible bands, for individual *C. goreaui* growth rates. Candidate headings give pseudo-BMA weights; the purple line marks the model-averaged N(S)EC. Black points are treatment means from the Negri et al. (2021) workbook (CC BY-NC 3.0 Australia). TAH concentrations are relative to the source-coded control."}
knitr::include_graphics("vignette-fig-example10-marine-fits.png")
```

The model-averaged curve rises modestly before declining (Figure 3). Its N(S)EC was 2.07 mg/L TAH (1.74–2.42), with an estimated 8.4% decline at that median concentration (−0.1–18.7%). The average combines the `neclin` NEC with NSECs from four hormesis equations [@fisher2023ieam]. The smooth `ecxhormebc5` equation received 0.776 of the weight and `neclinhorme` received 0.191, so the average follows the smooth declining limb while retaining the threshold equation's evidence for a low-dose rise.

## Lettuce biomass

The lettuce mean at 1 mg/L was 1.250, approximately 28% above the control mean of 0.979 (Figure 2). The three retained threshold equations reproduced a low-dose rise, with fitted means of 1.095–1.154 at 1 mg/L; the smooth `ecxhormebc4` equation showed less of the rise (Figure 4). Their NSECs are all interpolated between treatments and have nonzero paired ECNSECs (Table 3).

| Equation | Pseudo-BMA weight | Fitted control | Fitted mean at 1 mg/L | NSEC, mg/L (95% CrI) | ECNSEC, % (95% CrI) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `neclinhorme` | 0.238 | 1.044 | 1.095 | 5.90 (2.98–10.20) | 20.7 (4.7–31.0) |
| `nechorme` | 0.435 | 1.026 | 1.154 | 4.74 (2.95–8.11) | 19.0 (4.1–29.3) |
| `nechorme4` | 0.259 | 1.026 | 1.148 | 4.85 (2.73–8.66) | 20.2 (4.4–30.1) |
| `ecxhormebc4` | 0.068 | 1.055 | 1.058 | 6.97 (2.89–13.93) | 23.0 (4.9–35.0) |
| **Model average** | — | — | — | **5.07 (2.90–9.90)** | **20.0 (4.2–30.2)** |

Table 3. Posterior medians and 95% credible intervals for lettuce shoot biomass. The model-average row combines the four retained equations with their pseudo-BMA weights; its ECNSEC is paired with the sampled NSEC. The observed means were 0.979 at the control and 1.250 at 1 mg/L.

```{r lettuce-fit-plot, echo=FALSE, out.width="100%", fig.cap="Figure 4. Model-averaged fitted mean and four retained candidate equations (brown), with 95% credible bands, for lettuce biomass. Candidate headings give pseudo-BMA weights; the purple line marks the model-averaged NSEC. Pale points are individual observations and black points are treatment means."}
knitr::include_graphics("vignette-fig-example10-lettuce-fit.png")
```

The model-averaged NSEC was 5.07 mg/L (2.90–9.90), between the 3.2 and 10 mg/L treatments, with a paired ECNSEC of 20.0% (4.2–30.2%). The three threshold equations accounted for 0.932 of the model weight, so their shared low-dose rise is apparent in the averaged curve (Figure 4). The control has only two observations, which contributes to the broad intervals around the estimated location and effect.

# Interpretation

For both endpoints, the fitted response rises above the control before falling. `bayesnec` distinguishes the peak parameter of a threshold hormesis equation from the no-effect estimate read on its descending limb. An NSEC can have a nonzero ECNSEC, as a NOEC can, while its fitted curve locates it between tested concentrations [@fisherfox2023; @fisher2023ieam]. The marine N(S)EC and lettuce NSEC therefore describe the return towards and below the unexposed response after stimulation. A guideline assessment would also consider exposure measurements and the biological meaning of the measured endpoint.

# Reproducibility

The marine observations are imported locally by `data-raw/hormesis_negri.R` and are not redistributed with the package. Initial fits used seed 570 on `bayesnec` commits `a1fcf719`, `0b8acbdd` and `98c76be8`. The sampler refits and Figures 3 and 4 used the same seed with the fitting code at commit `afb1f206` (R 4.6.1, `brms` 2.23.0 and `rstan` 2.32.7). The [research compendium](https://github.com/open-AIMS/hormesis-nsec-compendium) contains `analysis/hormesis_case_fits.R`, which saves the screened model sets and exact lettuce LOO scores. `data-raw/hormesis_fit_plots.R` recreates the figures and estimate summary from those saved objects.

